[Music]  [Music]  [Applause]  surprisingly enough  i suppose there is uh my philosophical  interests  uh and throughout my life and the long  career  have centered on the question of  disagreement and difference if you  wanted to find this sociological or  personal reason for that it goes back to  my history as  someone belonging to a multiplicity of  cultures and languages  political systems and i have always  perceived these differences  and disagreements in the background of  my life and have  wanted to find philosophical as well as  personal answers to the difficult  questions that they raised  so let's take relativism first  one of the main motivations for  relativism going  back to protagoras was  the fact that there are so many  disagreements among people people  disagree about whether the wind is cold  or  hot people disagree about politics they  disagree about  ethics they disagree about what is there  out there in the world  etc etc and some of these disagreements  are  intractable they cannot be resolved  uh how are we going to respond  to these intractable disagreements well  relativism is one response  there are disagreements we all know that  so you can either say that only one side  is right  or you can say that both sides of the  disagreements are right  one according to his or her cultural  and sociological and framework and  background  and the next according to his or her own  very different  cultural or social or cognitive  framework  so that solves in some ways the problem  of disagreement  uh now how does that relate to trust  okay so when i was thinking about  disagreement in this  wider context with the  start of political upheavals in western  democracies  with brexit and trump etc etc  the question of disagreement among  scientists pure disagreement  something that as a philosopher i was  familiar with  started to have a very direct influence  on  our lives and our own choices and on  public discourse in general  so i started looking at that particular  issue  through a research project called when  experts disagree  the project was well multi-disciplinary  between a scientist and the philosopher  but had a practical dimension we just  wanted to see how  we can understand the phenomenon of  disagreement among scientists  and then more or less all hell broke  loose  and we had a  series of backlashes against experts  whether it was on climate science or  gastric  economies etc and i began  looking into the question in much more  detail  and in a more practical sense in every  sense that was relevant to  empirical work being done in these areas  and at that stage  i realized that the issue of  disagreement about  science or disagreement among experts  actually boils down to question of trust  trusting experts  which of these disagreeing sides do you  trust hence my work with relativism  ended me in in in work in  on trust  [Music]  the project when experts disagree was  fundamentally just about that about  instances of disagreement between  experts experts with  similar background and similar training  and  similar understanding of issues uh and  they're coming up with very different  uh conclusions they were particularly  interested at that time in question of  climate science  but but in fact the series of studies  that we did showed that  or supported the view that there is  relatively little disagreement among  climate scientists  or other case study was uh disagreement  among astrophysicists  they found out that there was far  greater disagreement among  astrophysicists than climate scientists  but astrophysics does not have any  political consequences so no one pays  much attention to their work  but then gradually as i said this this  uh  study culminated in the question of  trust in  science and currently uh we  are i'm running a large  european commission project called  peritea  or policy expertise and trust in action  about multi-disciplinary project with 11  participating countries  where we are focusing on the  markers for trusting experts  uh the public opinion on trusting  experts  and also steps to take to establish  trust for the relationships between  the public policy makers and the experts  so so what we think now what we are  focusing now  is not just question of trust but  but we have turned it into the question  of trustworthiness  we should not blindly trust anyone so  that  that applies to all areas we should only  trust those who are trustworthy  so the question we are asking what are  the markers what are the conditions  what are the criteria of trustworthiness  and not just how and when we trust  others including experts so there are  some  standard well-known criteria for  trustworthiness  efficiency reliability honesty  etc etc and those those criteria  are established on basis of track record  background education uh performance  and so on but also we think that looking  at the  political scene today  [Music]  many experts and policy makers may have  underestimated the role of  firstly emotions or affects and secondly  role of values in establishing trust and  trustworthiness  various studies uh including those done  by one of  my colleagues in the project bobby duffy  at king's college  london have shown that people actually  perceive  trustworthiness in terms of not just  these formal criteria of competence and  uh track record etc but also in terms of  group  values and group emotions trust involves  a feeling a sense of vulnerability when  you trust someone you're  taking a risk you make yourself open to  betrayal  trust always is accompanied by the  possibility of betrayal  and it's that possibility that should be  taken account  in the relationships between people  or to be trusted or wish to be trusted  and people who do the trusting  and i think maybe somewhat  controversially that this  applies to experts just as much to our  parents and friends and children  etc  [Music]  well some people think that we don't  really  we can't talk about trust in experts as  susan howie uh excellent philosopher  working on trust in  edinburgh thinks that we should just  rely on talk about reliance on experts  as groups rather than individuals they  think that that  experts or trust is a question of uh  a relationship between individuals  rather than groups when it comes to  experts the way expertise works  at least in policy-making conditions uh  it's groups of experts that give policy  advice but you see that in  their current circumstances probate 19  and the question is can we really talk  about  trusting groups or should we only talk  about  trusting the individuals within groups  also in various psychological and  political studies of  expertise there has been very little  attention  to the question of affect or emotions  and the  question of values up to now we have  been doing  uh quite a wide-ranging survey of these  views  and we don't find much discussion of  these affective and  normative or evaluative considerations  in approaches to trust by political  scientists  and even a time psychologist so it's  controversial in that sense as well that  not many people have done it  [Music]  so our project has three steps the first  step and last  step you're currently engaged in is  purely theoretical  we are looking at philosophical  sociological and psychological  literature on  trust in experts the second phase is  empirical  we are and that's going to start quite  soon we are going to do surveys  in normal ways in ways that have not  been done before to at least to some  extent  and also lab experiments  using behavioral economics approaches  and then once we have the results of all  of these  will enter the third phase and my view  in a way  this is the most important phase where  we try  to establish direct linkages between  experts  and the general public and policy makers  through the so-called minifora or  citizens assemblies  at small scale the strategy here  is to bring groups of ordinary lay  people  together with experts place them in one  to  in direct contact with each other and  to start conversations between them and  to try  and see if this helps in establishing  trust  between the experts and the known  experts  i think this is the way to go to  strengthen democracy  in all sorts of areas it had the the  steps have been taken quite successfully  in ireland  when it came to the very difficult  abortion referendum and the equal  marriage referendum  and i think we can do the same for  smaller but equally important questions  of  the role of science in our public life  [Music]  democracy is supposed to  take into account or be affected by the  voice of the people  one of the issues that exists there i  have to admit it about  the role of experts in uh policy  decision  uh is that experts  are not elected they are not  one of the people by definition they  they know more about  a particular subject matter than the  general  population and yet they are to have a  direct  impact on our lives and on policy  decisions that will affect our life  so a citizen assembly in that particular  instance  in the case of experts and policy  decisions  would allow the voice of the people to  be heard  more directly it will allow  dialogue between policy makers and  the general public in a very in a live  an ongoing fashion citizen assemblies  are  supposed to be based on conversations uh  rather than a small group making  decisions  and then putting those decisions to vote  so it's a way of  overcoming some of the deficits of a  democratic system  when that democratic system is being  implemented  in very large and technologically  advanced societies  in particular it's a way of establishing  a connection  between people and the governance  or the system of governance in place  that's why it can be applied to all  sorts of issues all sorts of questions  from school to large and it has proven  to be quite effective  until such time that the more effective  method is found  well no that that that would be exactly  the wrong way to go  and that will give credence to the  uh objections of the populists uh  political leaders to relying on  expertise  uh to to have a plutocracy or a system  where  experts are governing even some a few  aspects of our lives let alone all  aspects  would be a form of dictatorship actually  even though it might be an enlightened  form of dictatorship but once you cut  the voice  of the general public then you don't  have a democracy  anymore and there's no reason to think  that they will make the best judgments  they may be quite versed in their areas  of expertise  but even experts themselves would admit  that it's give and take  opposition disagreement putting your  ideas to test  those are the best ways of improving  scientific expertise and also the best  way of improving  governance in general  interestingly i think the horrendous  events of the pan  events of the pandemic have improved  so far the relationship between experts  and the general public even  michael gove who famously questioned the  role of experts and said who needs  experts  now relies on experts now there are  dangers to that  i think some governments they have seen  examples of that in many different  countries  do blame their errors on experts  secondly experts  in this in this case in case of  pandemics medical experts  virologists etc like all scientists  are prone to errors uh what the advice  they give  cannot be 100 correct and  their heirs will have her and could have  horrendous consequences so they are  placed in a particular particularly  difficult  position right now because  there is so much uncertainty around  this new virus  any advice they might present is going  to be  inevitably provisional but will have  negative consequences once it's proven  to be wrong  so so we are we are witnessing a very  interesting moment  in the connect relationship between  experts and the general public  on the one hand the general public more  than ever  is showing trust in experts and this has  been backed by various surveys that have  been taken  around in european countries and in  america on the question of kobe 19 and  trusted  experts on the other hand the  position of the experts is incredibly  precarious right now  because they can't be turned into  skateboards and also  they are inevitably going to give some  incorrect  advice or some over cautious advice  and then going to be blamed for their  errors  and that can have long-term impact on  the connection between  experts and the lay people  for more debates talks and interviews  subscribe today  to the institute of art and ideas at iai  tv  you

